\title[The universal cap $4/3$ for residue-dial speedups]{The Universal Cap $4/3$ for Residue-Dial Speedups\\
\normalsize An exact single-pass law, its converse for the congruence stratum,\\
\normalsize and the accounting boundary at $2$}

\author{Aristotle}
\date{22 August 2026}

\begin{abstract}
We study the maximal speedup that congruence information can confer on a single-pass scanning search --- the setting in which a factoring or divisor-search algorithm is told, for free, whether the residue class of its target modulo $M$ lies in a prescribed set $K$. We prove an \emph{exact} law: if $\theta = |K|/\varphi(M)$ is the density of the filter, the expected cost of the dial-aware scan, normalised by the unfiltered baseline, is exactly $1-\theta+\theta^{2}$, so that $\Speedup(\theta) = 1/(1-\theta+\theta^{2})$. Completing the square yields the \emph{universal cap} $\Speedup \le 4/3$, with equality if and only if $\theta = 1/2$; trivial filters give exactly $1$. The law depends on $K$ only through $|K|$, from which we deduce a family of converse statements: no character-theoretic, symbol-theoretic, positional, or interval structure can beat a plain half-density set; reading a dial against either factor of a semiprime gives identically the same speedup; and composing dials on coprime moduli via the Chinese Remainder Theorem multiplies densities and therefore inherits the same cap. The last point separates two currencies: the \emph{capacity} $\log_2(1/\theta)$ of a battery is unbounded, whereas the \emph{work} $\log_2\Speedup$ it purchases is at most $\log_2(4/3) = 0.41504\ldots$ bits and tends to $0$ as capacity grows. We then locate, precisely, the discrepancy between $4/3$ and the barrier $2$ that the literature asks about: under expected-position accounting we prove an optimality lemma for arbitrary schedules and deduce a strict barrier of $2$, approached but never attained; under a multi-symbol generalisation we prove an order-free cost identity and the cap hierarchy $2r/(r+1)$; and we identify the exact boundary --- if the dial's answer permits the algorithm to \emph{skip} rejected blocks, the cost becomes $\sum_i\theta_i^2$ and a balanced $r$-symbol reveal buys exactly $r$.
\end{abstract}

