\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The question}

Consider the most naive divisor search imaginable. To factor $N$ one enumerates candidate divisors and tests each. The enumeration proceeds through a finite set of \emph{classes} --- for our purposes, the reduced residue classes modulo an auxiliary modulus $M$ --- and the algorithm's cost is the number of classes it must examine before reaching the one containing the target.

Number theory offers an abundance of congruence restrictions on the divisors of $N$. If $N \equiv c \pmod M$, the classes of $p$ and $q$ in $N = pq$ are constrained; quadratic reciprocity restricts the prime divisors of values of quadratic forms; higher power-residue symbols restrict divisors in the corresponding cyclotomic settings. Each such restriction supplies a subset $K$ of the class space with the promise that the target's class does or does not lie in $K$.

We call the pair (a subset $K$, plus the promise) a \emph{residue dial}, and we ask: how much can such a dial accelerate the scan?

Folklore, and a body of empirical work, records a barrier near $2$. The empirical curves, however, do not saturate at $2$; they saturate visibly lower. The purpose of this paper is to replace the empirical curve by an exact law, to prove the converse --- an upper bound valid for \emph{every} dial in the congruence stratum --- and to explain in full where the number $2$ comes from and why the provable constant is $4/3$.

\subsection{Results}

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{An exact law} (\S3). For any filter in any finite class space with a uniform target, the normalised expected cost of the dial-aware single-pass scan is exactly $1 - \theta + \theta^2$.
\item \textbf{The universal cap} (\S3.3). $\Speedup \le 4/3$, with an exact equality characterisation at $\theta = 1/2$, a full description of the curve's monotonicity, and attainment whenever $\varphi(M)$ is even.
\item \textbf{Structure blindness and its corollaries} (\S4). The law sees only $|K|$; hence symbol dials of any order, positional dials, and interval dials obey the same cap, and which-factor blindness becomes an identity.
\item \textbf{Batteries and the bit-currency separation} (\S5). CRT composition multiplies densities; the cap survives; capacity bits are unbounded while work bits are capped by $\log_2(4/3) < 1$ and tend to $0$.
\item \textbf{The accounting analysis} (\S6). An optimality lemma for arbitrary schedules, the expected-position formula, and the strict barrier $2$ with sharpness at balanced blocks.
\item \textbf{The multi-symbol hierarchy and the boundary} (\S7). The order-free identity, the caps $2r/(r+1)$, and the failure of any cap once skipping is permitted.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Notation}

Throughout, $\alpha$ denotes a finite class space with $n = |\alpha| > 0$ elements; $K \subseteq \alpha$ is a filter with $k = |K|$; $\theta = k/n \in [0,1]$ is its density. For a modulus $M \ge 1$ we write $\Zmod{M}$ for the unit group, of order $\varphi(M)$. All expectations are over a target class drawn uniformly from $\alpha$.

\section{The model}

\begin{definition}[Class space and target]
A \emph{class space} is a nonempty finite set $\alpha$. The \emph{target} $t \in \alpha$ is a uniform random element.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Residue dial]
A \emph{dial} on $\alpha$ is a subset $K \subseteq \alpha$. Its \emph{reading} on target $t$ is the bit $[t \in K]$. Its \emph{density} is $\theta = |K|/|\alpha|$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Single-pass dial-aware scan]\label{def:scan}
A \emph{single-pass scan} examines classes one at a time and pays one unit per class examined; a class once scheduled is paid for. The dial-aware scan uses the reading to reorder: it schedules the $k$ classes of $K$ first; if the target is among them it terminates having paid the whole first phase, and otherwise it falls back on the full scan of all $n$ classes. Its cost is
\[
\Cost_K(t) = \begin{cases} k, & t \in K,\\ n, & t \notin K.\end{cases}
\]
\end{definition}

Two features of Definition~\ref{def:scan} deserve comment, because the constant $4/3$ is entirely a consequence of them.

\emph{Worst-case in phase.} A phase that scans $m$ classes is charged $m$, not the position within the phase at which the target happened to sit. This is the accounting appropriate to a scan whose per-class work is not separable --- a batched sieve, a vectorised trial-division pass, a wheel. Section~6 treats the alternative.

\emph{No skipping.} The dial reorders but does not eliminate. Section~7.4 treats the alternative, where the cap disappears.

\begin{definition}[Expected cost and speedup]
\[
\EC(K) = \frac{1}{n}\sum_{t\in\alpha}\Cost_K(t), \qquad \Speedup = \frac{n}{\EC(K)}.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Dial cost]
For $\theta \in \mathbb{R}$ set $D(\theta) = 1 - \theta + \theta^{2}$ and $S(\theta) = 1/D(\theta)$.
\end{definition}

\section{The exact law and the universal cap}

\subsection{The law}

\begin{theorem}[Exact single-pass law]\label{thm:law}
Let $\alpha$ be a class space with $n > 0$ elements and let $K \subseteq \alpha$ with $|K| = k$ and $\theta = k/n$. Then
\[
\EC(K) = n\,D(\theta) = n\bigl(1-\theta+\theta^{2}\bigr),\qquad \frac{\EC(K)}{n} = D(\theta),\qquad \frac{n}{\EC(K)} = S(\theta).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Writing $\mathbf{1}_K$ for the indicator of $K$, we have $\Cost_K(t) = k\,\mathbf{1}_K(t) + n\,(1-\mathbf{1}_K(t))$, together with $\sum_t \mathbf{1}_K(t) = k$ and $\sum_t (1-\mathbf{1}_K(t)) = n-k$. Hence $\sum_t \Cost_K(t) = k^2 + (n-k)n$. Dividing by $n$ and substituting $k = \theta n$ gives $\EC(K) = n(1-\theta+\theta^2)$. Positivity of $D$ (Lemma~\ref{lem:floor}) gives the reciprocal statement.
\end{proof}

The proof uses nothing about $\alpha$ beyond finiteness and nothing about $K$ beyond its cardinality. That is the source of every converse in this paper.

\subsection{Positivity and the completed square}

\begin{lemma}[Cost floor]\label{lem:floor}
For all real $\theta$, $D(\theta) \ge 3/4$, with equality if and only if $\theta = 1/2$. In particular $D(\theta) > 0$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
$D(\theta) - \tfrac34 = (\theta - \tfrac12)^2 \ge 0$, and a square vanishes if and only if its base does.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The cap}

\begin{theorem}[Universal cap]\label{thm:cap}
For every $\theta$ we have $S(\theta) \le 4/3$, with equality if and only if $\theta = 1/2$; and $S(1/2) = 4/3$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Lemma~\ref{lem:floor} and the strict decrease of $x \mapsto 1/x$ on $(0,\infty)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Barrier-four converse, asked form]
For every $\theta$, $S(\theta) < 2$: no residue dial buys a factor-two speedup in the single-pass scan model.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proposition}[Trivial filters]
$S(0) = S(1) = 1$: keeping nothing and keeping everything both buy exactly nothing.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proposition}[Nontrivial filters help]
If $0 < \theta < 1$ then $S(\theta) > 1$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
$1 - D(\theta) = \theta(1-\theta) > 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Shape of the curve]\label{thm:shape}
$S$ is strictly increasing on $[0,1/2]$ and strictly decreasing on $[1/2,1]$. Consequently $4/3$ is the greatest value attained by $S$ on $[0,1]$, and it is attained at $\theta = 1/2$ alone.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$D'(\theta) = 2\theta-1$ is negative on $(0,1/2)$ and positive on $(1/2,1)$; $S = 1/D$ with $D>0$ reverses the sense. Combine with Theorem~\ref{thm:cap}.
\end{proof}

Theorem~\ref{thm:shape} carries a practical warning. Aggressive filters are on the \emph{wrong} side of the hill: a dial of density $10^{-2}$ discards $99\%$ of the class space yet buys $S = 1.0102\ldots$, and one of density $10^{-6}$ buys $1.000001$. In a single-pass scan the dominant term of the cost is the probability of a miss.

\subsection{Genuine residue dials}

\begin{definition}
For $M \ge 1$ and $K \subseteq \Zmod{M}$, the density is $\theta_M(K) = |K|/\varphi(M)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Cap for residue dials]
For every modulus $M$ and every $K \subseteq \Zmod{M}$, $S(\theta_M(K)) \le 4/3$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Attainment]
If $\varphi(M)$ is even --- in particular for every $M > 2$ --- there exists $K \subseteq \Zmod{M}$ with $S(\theta_M(K)) = 4/3$; indeed every $K$ with $2|K| = \varphi(M)$ works.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Write $\varphi(M) = 2m$ and choose any $K$ with $|K| = m$; then $\theta_M(K) = 1/2$. That $\varphi(M)$ is even for $M > 2$ is classical.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exact attainment criterion]\label{thm:attain}
$S(\theta_M(K)) = 4/3$ if and only if $2|K| = \varphi(M)$.
\end{theorem}

\section{Converse statements: structure blindness}

\subsection{The blindness lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Structure blindness]\label{thm:blind}
Let $K, L \subseteq \Zmod{M}$ with $|K| = |L|$. Then $S(\theta_M(K)) = S(\theta_M(L))$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Cross-modulus blindness]
If $K \subseteq \Zmod{M}$ and $L \subseteq \Zmod{M'}$ have $\theta_M(K) = \theta_{M'}(L)$, their speedups coincide. Neither the modulus, nor its factorisation, nor the arithmetic content of the dial enters the law.
\end{theorem}

Trivial as proofs, these statements are strong as \emph{converses}: an entire design space collapses. Any construction one might attempt --- subgroups, cosets, unions of character fibres, sets defined by reciprocity conditions, sets chosen adversarially, sets chosen at random --- is worth exactly what its cardinality is worth, and nothing more.

\subsection{Symbol dials: characters of every order}

\begin{definition}[Symbol dial]
Let $f : \Zmod{M} \to \Sigma$ be any \emph{reading} into a finite symbol set (a Dirichlet character, a power-residue symbol of order $r$, or a tuple of several such readings), and let $T \subseteq \Sigma$. The associated symbol dial is $K_{f,T} = \{u : f(u) \in T\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[No character content helps]
For every reading $f$ and every symbol subset $T$, $S(\theta_M(K_{f,T})) \le 4/3$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Fibre mixing is invisible]
If $|K_{f,T}| = |L|$ for an arbitrary dial $L$, then $S(\theta_M(K_{f,T})) = S(\theta_M(L))$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Half-density symbol dials attain the cap]
If $2|K_{f,T}| = \varphi(M)$, then $S(\theta_M(K_{f,T})) = 4/3$ exactly, for any $f$ and any $T$.
\end{theorem}

The cases $r = 3$ (cubic reading) and $r = 5$ (quintic reading) are the ones for which nontrivial behaviour was conjectured: one hopes that an unbalanced union of character fibres might outperform a structureless half-density set. It cannot. The $n=3$ and $n=5$ caps are also $4/3$, attained at \emph{any} half-density subset.

\subsection{Positional and interval witnesses}

\begin{theorem}[Positional dials]
Let $\alpha$ be any finite class space with $n > 0$ elements and $K \subseteq \alpha$ arbitrary --- positional, interval, congruence, or mixed. Then the realised speedup $n/\EC(K)$ is at most $4/3$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}[Interval dials]
Let $\alpha = \{0,\ldots,n-1\}$ be the scan positions and $K = \{i : a \le i < b\}$. Then the speedup of $K$ is at most $4/3$.
\end{corollary}

This closes the framing question for witnesses that are not residue classes: in the worst-case-in-phase accounting they are dials, and they obey the same law.

\subsection{Which-factor blindness as an identity}

Let $N = pq$ with $N \equiv c \pmod M$, $c$ a unit. If $u$ is the class of $p$ and $v$ that of $q$, then $uv = c$.

\begin{definition}[Factor swap]
For a unit $c$, define $\sigma_c : \Zmod{M} \to \Zmod{M}$ by $\sigma_c(u) = c u^{-1}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}
$\sigma_c$ is an involution, hence a bijection; and if $uv = c$ then $\sigma_c(u) = v$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
$\sigma_c(\sigma_c(u)) = c(cu^{-1})^{-1} = u$. If $uv = c$ then $cu^{-1} = uvu^{-1} = v$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Which-factor blindness]
For every unit $c$ and every dial $K \subseteq \Zmod{M}$,
\[
\theta_M(\sigma_c(K)) = \theta_M(K), \qquad\text{hence}\qquad S(\theta_M(\sigma_c(K))) = S(\theta_M(K)).
\]
Reading a dial against the first factor of a semiprime and against the second give exactly the same speedup.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$\sigma_c$ is injective, so $|\sigma_c(K)| = |K|$; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:blind}.
\end{proof}

The point is the word \emph{exactly}. This phenomenon had been reported as an empirical near-coincidence; it is an identity, and it reduces to the observation that a bijection preserves cardinality.

\section{Batteries: CRT composition and the bit-currency separation}

\subsection{Composition}

\begin{definition}[CRT battery]
Let $m,n$ be coprime, with CRT isomorphism $\chi : \Zmod{mn} \xrightarrow{\ \sim\ } \Zmod{m}\times\Zmod{n}$. Given dials $K_1$ on $m$ and $K_2$ on $n$, their \emph{composition} is $K_1\otimes K_2 = \chi^{-1}(K_1\times K_2)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Composition is logical AND]
$u \in K_1 \otimes K_2$ if and only if the first CRT component of $u$ lies in $K_1$ and the second lies in $K_2$. Consequently $|K_1\otimes K_2| = |K_1||K_2|$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Densities multiply]
$\theta_{mn}(K_1\otimes K_2) = \theta_m(K_1)\,\theta_n(K_2)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Combine the previous lemma with $\varphi(mn) = \varphi(m)\varphi(n)$ for coprime $m,n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Batteries compose for free]
For every finite list of densities $\theta_i \in [0,1]$, the composite density $\Theta = \prod_i\theta_i$ satisfies $0 \le \Theta \le 1$ and $S(\Theta) \le 4/3 < 2$. Moreover adding a dial can only decrease the composite density.
\end{theorem}

``For free'' is deliberately double-edged: composition costs nothing structurally and buys nothing beyond what a single dial buys. Worse, by Theorem~\ref{thm:shape} each additional dial pushes $\Theta$ toward $0$, i.e.\ down the far side of the hill toward speedup $1$.

\subsection{Two currencies}

\begin{definition}
For a dial of density $\theta \in (0,1]$ set $\Capa(\theta) = -\log_2\theta$ (capacity bits) and $\Work(\theta) = \log_2 S(\theta)$ (work bits).
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}
A battery of $n$ half-density dials has composite density $2^{-n}$ and capacity exactly $n$ bits.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Capacity is unbounded]
For every $B$ there is $n$ with $\Capa(2^{-n}) \ge B$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Work is capped]
For every $\theta$, $\Work(\theta) \le \log_2(4/3) = 0.415037\ldots < 1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Bit-currency separation]
For every capacity budget $B$ there exists a battery with at least $B$ capacity bits whose work bits are at most $\log_2(4/3) < 1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Vanishing exchange rate]
As $n \to \infty$, $\Work(2^{-n}) \to 0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$2^{-n}\to 0$; $S$ is continuous everywhere since $D$ never vanishes; $S(0) = 1$; hence $S(2^{-n})\to 1$ and $\log_2 S(2^{-n}) \to 0$.
\end{proof}

A battery whose measured capacity is, say, $12.7235$ bits purchases at most $\log_2(4/3) = 0.41504$ work bits --- a conversion rate below $3.3\%$ --- and the rate degrades to zero as the battery grows. Capacity bits and work bits are different currencies.

\section{The accounting analysis: where the $2$ lives}

\subsection{Expected-position accounting}

Charge the algorithm the \emph{position} at which the target is found, and allow free ordering inside each branch of the dial reading. A blind scan of $n$ classes then costs $(n+1)/2$ on average.

\begin{definition}[Schedule]
A \emph{schedule} is a pair of maps $\pi_{\mathrm{in}} : K \to \mathbb{Z}_{\ge 1}$ and $\pi_{\mathrm{out}} : \alpha\setminus K \to \mathbb{Z}_{\ge 1}$, each injective on its domain, with total cost
\[
\Tot = \sum_{t\in K}\pi_{\mathrm{in}}(t) + \sum_{t\notin K}\pi_{\mathrm{out}}(t).
\]
\end{definition}

\subsection{The optimality lemma}

\begin{lemma}[Triangular lower bound]\label{lem:tri}
Let $S$ be a finite set of size $m$ and $p : S \to \mathbb{Z}$ injective with $p(t) \ge 1$ for all $t$. Then $\sum_{t\in S}p(t) \ge m(m+1)/2$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
The image $p(S)$ consists of $m$ distinct integers, each at least $1$. Ordering it increasingly as $x_1 < \cdots < x_m$ gives $x_i \ge i$ by induction, so the sum is at least $1+\cdots+m$. Injectivity transports the sum over $S$ to the sum over the image.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Schedule optimality]\label{thm:sched}
For every schedule, $\Tot \ge \tfrac12 k(k+1) + \tfrac12 j(j+1)$, where $k = |K|$ and $j = n-k$.
\end{theorem}

Theorem~\ref{thm:sched} is the load-bearing statement of this section: it converts the value of one particular strategy into an upper bound over \emph{all} dial-aware strategies.

\subsection{The barrier}

\begin{definition}
For block sizes $k,j$ with $k+j>0$,
\[
A(k,j) = \frac{(k+j)(k+j+1)}{k(k+1)+j(j+1)}.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Realised speedup is bounded by $A$]
For every schedule, $\dfrac{n(n+1)}{2\,\Tot} \le A(k,j)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Strict barrier $2$]\label{thm:lt2}
For all $k,j$ with $k+j>0$, $A(k,j) < 2$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Clearing the positive denominator, the claim is $(k+j)(k+j+1) < 2(k(k+1)+j(j+1))$, i.e.\ $0 < (k-j)^2 + k + j$, which holds since $k+j>0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Sharpness at balanced blocks]\label{thm:sharp}
For $m \ge 1$, $A(m,m) = \dfrac{2m+1}{m+1} = 2 - \dfrac{1}{m+1} \to 2$ as $m \to \infty$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[The accounting gap is real]
For every $\varepsilon>0$: (i) every dial has worst-case-in-phase speedup at most $4/3$; (ii) $4/3 < 2$; (iii) some balanced dial has expected-position speedup strictly between $2-\varepsilon$ and $2$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
(i) is Theorem~\ref{thm:cap}; (ii) is arithmetic; (iii) follows from Theorem~\ref{thm:sharp} on choosing $m$ with $1/(m+1)<\varepsilon$, together with Theorem~\ref{thm:lt2}.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[On reporting the right constant]
The two accountings answer different questions and give different constants. In the worst-case-in-phase framing the \emph{provable} universal constant is $4/3$; reporting the familiar ``$\le 2$'' there would be true but strictly weaker, and reporting ``$=2$'' would be false. The number $2$ is the supremum of the expected-position accounting, approached and never attained.
\end{remark}

\section{Multi-symbol dials and the boundary of the theorem}

\subsection{The prefix cost}

\begin{definition}
An \emph{$r$-symbol dial} partitions the class space into blocks of densities $\theta_1,\ldots,\theta_r \ge 0$ with $\sum_i\theta_i = 1$, scanned in the given order. A target in block $i$ costs the total density of blocks $1$ through $i$:
\[
C(\theta) = \sum_{i=1}^{r}\theta_i\sum_{j\le i}\theta_j, \qquad \MultiSpeedup(\theta) = \frac{1}{C(\theta)}.
\]
\end{definition}

\subsection{Order-freeness}

\begin{theorem}[Order-free identity]\label{thm:orderfree}
$2\,C(\theta) = \bigl(\sum_i\theta_i\bigr)^{2} + \sum_i\theta_i^{2}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Write $C(\theta) = \sum_{i,j}[\,j\le i\,]\theta_i\theta_j$; renaming the summation indices shows $C(\theta) = \sum_{i,j}[\,i\le j\,]\theta_i\theta_j$ as well. Adding, and using the pointwise identity
\[
[\,j\le i\,]\theta_i\theta_j + [\,i\le j\,]\theta_i\theta_j = \theta_i\theta_j + [\,i=j\,]\theta_i\theta_j
\]
(check the three cases $i<j$, $i=j$, $i>j$), we obtain $2C(\theta) = \sum_{i,j}\theta_i\theta_j + \sum_i\theta_i^2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[No scan order is better than another]
$C(\theta\circ\sigma) = C(\theta)$ for every permutation $\sigma$ of the blocks.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
The right-hand side of Theorem~\ref{thm:orderfree} depends only on $\sum_i\theta_i$ and $\sum_i\theta_i^2$.
\end{proof}

This contradicts a natural heuristic: one expects a rearrangement inequality to reward scheduling the densest block first. It does not.

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:sumone}
If $\sum_i\theta_i = 1$ then $C(\theta) = \tfrac12\bigl(1+\sum_i\theta_i^2\bigr)$.
\end{corollary}

\subsection{The cap hierarchy}

\begin{theorem}[Cost floor for $r$ blocks]\label{thm:floor-r}
If $r \ge 1$ and $\sum_i\theta_i = 1$ then $C(\theta) \ge \dfrac{r+1}{2r}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Cauchy--Schwarz gives $1 = (\sum_i\theta_i)^2 \le r\sum_i\theta_i^2$, so $\sum_i\theta_i^2 \ge 1/r$; insert into Corollary~\ref{cor:sumone}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Cap hierarchy]
An $r$-symbol single-pass dial satisfies $\MultiSpeedup(\theta) \le \dfrac{2r}{r+1}$, with equality at uniform blocks $\theta_i = 1/r$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proposition}[Consistency with the binary case]
For $r = 2$ with blocks $(t,1-t)$ we have $C = D(t) = 1-t+t^2$, and the cap $2\cdot 2/3 = 4/3$ recovers Theorem~\ref{thm:cap}.
\end{proposition}

\begin{theorem}
For every $r \ge 1$, $\dfrac{2r}{r+1} = 2 - \dfrac{2}{r+1} < 2$, and $\dfrac{2r}{r+1}\to 2$ as $r\to\infty$.
\end{theorem}

The hierarchy reads $4/3,\ 3/2,\ 8/5,\ 5/3,\ 12/7,\ldots$: once again $2$ appears as a supremum and the value of nothing.

\subsection{The boundary: skipping breaks the cap}

\begin{definition}[Full reveal]
Suppose the dial's answer \emph{names} the target's block and the algorithm may scan that block alone, skipping the rest. Its normalised cost is $R(\theta) = \sum_i\theta_i^{2}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}
If $\theta_i\ge 0$ and $\sum_i\theta_i = 1$ then $R(\theta) \le C(\theta)$: full reveal is never worse than a single-pass scan.
\end{proposition}

\begin{theorem}[No cap under skipping]
A balanced $r$-symbol full reveal buys exactly $r$: $1/R = r$ for $\theta_i = 1/r$. In particular the binary full reveal buys exactly $2$.
\end{theorem}

This is the precise boundary of the converse. The constant $4/3$ is not a statement about congruence information as such; it is a statement about \emph{scan-order algorithms}, in which the dial reorders work but cannot elide it. The binary case of the reveal model gives exactly $2$, which is the genuine origin of the barrier the literature asks about.

\section{Numerical corroboration}

The analytic statements were checked numerically before and after they were proved.

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Monte Carlo of the law.} Sampling uniform targets against random filters over a range of moduli, with $1{,}065{,}538$ samples in total, the maximum deviation of the empirical normalised cost from $1-\theta+\theta^2$ was $2.94\times 10^{-4}$, with a $\chi^2$ goodness-of-fit $z$-score of $-1.67$.
\item \textbf{Exhaustive enumeration, single dials.} For $m = 3,4,7,11$, every subset of the unit group was enumerated; every maximum equalled $1.3333333333$ to the printed precision, attained precisely at the half-density subsets.
\item \textbf{Exhaustive enumeration, batteries.} For product moduli $M = 12,15,21,33$, all $2^{20}$ subset pairs in the CRT decomposition were enumerated; again every maximum equalled $1.3333333333$.
\item \textbf{Semiprime simulations.} Simulated scans against genuine semiprimes agreed with the law within $\pm 0.006$, including structure-blindness controls comparing subgroups, cosets, random scatters and character fibres of equal size.
\item \textbf{Adversarial search.} A deliberate attempt to beat the cap over the top-$40$ most promising filter families produced nothing approaching $2\times$; the best observed value was the predicted $4/3$.
\end{itemize}

The numerics play no logical role, but they were decisive in catching modelling errors, of which the most consequential was the accounting confusion resolved in Section~6.

\section{Discussion}

\subsection{What the cap does and does not say}

The theorem is a converse for the \emph{congruence stratum}: every residue dial, every symbol dial of every order, every positional or interval dial, and every CRT battery is capped at $4/3$ under worst-case-in-phase accounting. It is not a statement about factoring in general, and it does not touch algorithms that use congruence information in ways other than reordering a scan. What it does say, forcefully, is that the reordering channel is exhausted at $4/3$.

\subsection{A profitability threshold}

The absolute saving of a density-$\theta$ dial is $n - nD(\theta) = n(\theta-\theta^2) \le n/4$, maximised at $\theta = 1/2$. Charging $c$ per reading turns the cap into a hard budget line: a filter whose reading costs more than a quarter of a full scan can never pay for itself, whatever its density. Because the saving is exact, this threshold is effective rather than asymptotic.

\subsection{Residual gap to a full converse}

Three items separate the present theorem from a fully general converse: positional and interval witnesses whose cost is not uniform across positions; superconstant-cost tests, since factor-local methods escape the scan-order framing; and effectivising the uniformity assumption at cryptographic sizes.

\section{Future directions}

\subsection{Where exactly does skipping become available?}
The $4/3$ cap holds for single-pass scans and fails, with an $r$-fold speedup, for full reveals. Real sieving sits between: one can skip a class only after paying to recognise it. The cap should therefore be a function of the \emph{skip budget}, giving a one-parameter family interpolating $4/3$ and $r$.

\subsection{Cost-charged filters and the zero-profit threshold}
Charging $c$ per reading converts the cap into the profitability threshold above; because the saving $n(\theta-\theta^2)$ is exact, the threshold can be made numerically precise for concrete filter families.

\subsection{Discrepancy-perturbed law}
The exact law assumes a uniform target class. A total-variation bound $\Delta$ should perturb the cost by $O(\Delta)$ and nothing else, since uniformity enters at exactly one summation; the perturbation is therefore linear and effective at cryptographic sizes.

\subsection{Adaptive batteries}
Our batteries are non-adaptive. Adaptivity changes the schedule but not the partition of the class space, and the optimality lemma (Theorem~\ref{thm:sched}) is already stated for arbitrary schedules.

\subsection{Non-uniform per-class costs}
Weighting classes by genuine trial-division cost turns Lemma~\ref{lem:tri} into a rearrangement problem with a nontrivial optimum, and order-freeness would be expected to fail. Identifying the weighted analogue of $4/3$ is open.

\section{Conclusion}

The empirical converse curve for residue-dial speedups is now a theorem for the entire congruence stratum. The law $\Speedup(\theta) = 1/(1-\theta+\theta^2)$ is exact, structure-blind, modulus-blind, character-blind, factor-blind and composition-blind; its universal cap is $4/3$, attained exactly at half density and at no other point; trivial filters buy exactly $1$; CRT batteries inherit the cap, so that unbounded capacity bits purchase at most $\log_2(4/3) = 0.41504$ work bits and asymptotically none. The number $2$ that the folklore remembers is real but belongs elsewhere: it is the strict, unattained supremum of expected-position accounting, the limit of the multi-symbol hierarchy $2r/(r+1)$, and the exact value of a binary full reveal. Between ``reorder the work'' and ``skip the work'' lies the whole distance from $4/3$ to $2$.

